Chris,
I've posted this back into the linux alpha group as I'm sure the PPC
people won't appreciate the alpha discussion. For people who don't
know, we operate Melbourne's free access internet bar. It's licensed
and every machine in the place runs linux. We even have some green
screen terminals for IRC and mail via pine.

Well I wanted, a non intel machine. I figured PPC or Alpha. I bought a 
dual PII with 233 CPUs. This despite Digitals Australia's generous
offer of a workstation 'a at cost.

I'd like to say I was very impressed that Digital were interested in
sponsering us as a linux/alpha reference site. I think this shows a
commitment to supporting linux on this platform.

Basically it came down to bucks. The PC came in at a little under
$3K, the Digital would have been $4.5K Not only cost. Firstly
cross compiling issues aside it makes sense for us to have a fast
machine to compile the few site specfic debian packages we need. We
have x86 linux experience, so transition was easy. Actually we had
hard disks already installed, so assembly and installation took less
than a day before we were operational.

Apart from cost, the alpha offered more power than we needed. I don't
know how they go in terms of reliability, but we purchased 'quality'
PC components, and in 18months of operation the PC's we already have
have worked well. I've seen the recent comparrison of P400's vs
Alphas. Um we have a dual P233, so we weren't really in the market for 
P400's either.

So in summary, Alpha didn't work for us because it was expensive, too
powerful and required a steeper learning curve than the
x86. Personally I'm a little disappointed, but think it was the right
decision. I'm staying subscribed to the alpha newsgroup, because I
think the alpha makes and excellent linux platform and encourages
'clever' problem solving people to us it. A small dig, but the PPC
group tends to be full of people asking if mklinux is better than
linuxPPC!

If I can ever get my hands on a 2nd hand 164LX or SX motherboard,
cheaply, then I'd gladly experiment with the alpha. But on my own time 
and not for my biz.

