Arguments you can pass go to the kernel (e.g. init=/bin/sh), or can go
to init itself (normally a runlevel, e.g. "single" or "3"). The SysVInit
scripts that come with Redhat are a problem (in my opinion) and try to
do too much if you go into single-user mode. You can bypass everything
by using "emergency" as the run-level. That isn't really much different
from simply saying init=/bin/sh, except I think if you exit the shell,
init will continue the normal boot process.

If you have a Redhat CD, you can just boot off of that, then use Alt-F2 to
get a shell. You could also run off of the CD with init=/bin/sh, then chroot
to the "live" disk system on the CD:

cd /live
usr/bin/chroot . /bin/sh

That will give you pretty much all the programs. Unfortunately, they left
/live/proc off of the RH5.0 CD, so you can't mount /proc in any useful way
(you can mount it, of course, but it won't be where programs that use it
expect to see it).

A read-only file system should have /etc/mtab be a symbolic link to
/proc/mounts; you could even have /proc/mounts exist, with an entry for
/dev/cdrom mounted at root (so df works before /proc is mounted).



