back here again....

I am attempting to recompile milo and finding that
/usr/src/linux/include/linux/fs.h and devices.c(in milo dir) have conflicting
declarations:

fs.h:
int block_read (yada)   //Note only one yada here, it has fewer params
int block_write(yada)

devices.c:
long block_read (yada yada)
long block_write (yada yada)

Ok not such a huge problem I thought for two reasons:
long=64 and int=64 (last I checked)

But as mentioned with the yadas above, they do not share the same number of
params.

Again, who really cares as milo and the kernel interact only sparsely when the
handoff occurs during bootup, thus milo's replication of all of the driver
services (which we really should fix one of these days..)

The problem comes when I comment out the read/write in fs.h and compile milo
again.   Of course the conflict is gone and the block reads/writes are now
presumably overloaded as required by milo.  Again, there should be no conflict
with the kernel reads and writes as they seem to be fully replicated in milo
for the sake of a quick and dirty solution.

When I boot this new milo up it gets to the floppy init and hangs (not
surprisingly where it should begin reading the SCSI drive (ie: block device).
this is in the milo boot not the kernel boot.

I am currently running a milo I compiled some time ago against this same
kernel so I know this works, but I do not rememeber what I did to get it to
work.  I thought I had seen this same problem before, but it disappeared when
I went to the newer sources.

ITS BACK!!!

What am i doing wrong here?

Thanks again,

/Mike


