Er, this is a mild back-flame.  Hope you don't mind me calling you a
dumb-ass.  :)

If you had set up your system correctly and read the kernel build
information, you would know about the following three symbolic links:

nemesis:~>ls -ld /usr/include/{asm,linux,scsi}
lrwxrwxrwx 1 root root 24 Mar 8 2018 /usr/include/asm -> ../src/linux/include/asm/
lrwxrwxrwx 1 root root 26 Mar 8 2018 /usr/include/linux -> ../src/linux/include/linux/
lrwxrwxrwx 1 root root 25 Mar 8 2018 /usr/include/scsi -> ../src/linux/include/scsi/
nemesis:~>

Of course, on my system the content of /usr/src/linux is the source
code to kernel version 2.1.103.  And yes, it is documented (although
i386-centric).  From linux/README, as part of the kernel source
installation:

> - Make sure your /usr/include/asm, /usr/include/linux, and
>   /usr/include/scsi directories are just symlinks to the kernel
>   sources:
>
>                cd /usr/include
>                rm -rf asm linux scsi
>                ln -s /usr/src/linux/include/asm-i386 asm
>                ln -s /usr/src/linux/include/linux linux
>                ln -s /usr/src/linux/include/scsi scsi

Of course, that should instead be 'ln -s /usr/src/linux-include/asm'
or 'ln -s /usr/src/linux-include/asm-alpha'.  So every time you update
your kernel, or install new kernel sources, you should either (a)
recreate these links per the installation instructions or (b) install
your kernel sources in such a way as those symbolic links will always
be correct.

So go Burn in Earth, Pink Boy!!  :)

