>>>>> "PT" == Paul Tomblin <ptomblin@canoe.xcski.com> writes:

    PT> Philosophical question for you: How do you decide whether to
    PT> upgrade your RedHat rpm to something in the contrib directory
    PT> on their ftp site?

Ever the masochist, I usually grab the source RPMs instead and compile
it myself.  I prefer to know when a program compiles cleanly, and just
snagging a binary RPM won't show me all those wonderful pointer-to-int
cast warnings that cause so many problems on the Alpha.

    PT> ...if this will cause problems for me later if RedHat puts
    PT> their own releases in "errata"...

I'd imagine that RedHat would increment the package version number if
changes need to be made before they use something from /pub/contrib in
something real.  If no changes need to be made, they might just
repackage the RPM (to add a PGP signature), but that shouldn't affect
upgrades at all, as long as the source code is the same.

    PT> Right now I'm looking mostly at the X_TGA driver and sendmail
    PT> 8.8, which have huge potential for being security holes since
    PT> they're basically run by root.

I've never heard of anybody being clobbered by something from
ftp.redhat.com:/pub/contrib/, but it could happen.  Of course, the
safest bet would be to grab the SRPM instead and compile it yourself
in your Copious Disk Space.  As I recall, the X device driver sources
aren't too big (only about 10 megs or so? maybe more because there
were fonts, I think), and sendmail shouldn't be bigger than a couple
of meg or so.

IIRC, Unpacking an SRPM is an unarchival process only.  No scripts are
run and your package database is not modified.  From there you'd be
able to see exactly what the contributed code does...

(Keep in mind, though, that I'm a mere neophyte when it comes to
system administration (heck, I've never even hurt anyone yet, let
alone killed someone!), and worse, a *gasp* student and a hobbyist, so
on a real network different rules may apply...)

