NOSPAMmbkennelNOSPAM@yahoo.com (Matt Kennel) wrote:

> On 9 May 1998 19:13:09 GMT, I wrote:

> :I know very well that there are codes where Digital Fortran currently does 
> :better than g77/egcs, but we're working on eliminating even that gap.
> 
> Will it be eliminated for elementary functions, most importantly 
sin/cos/exp?
> 
> This is where my performance has been very poor.   All FP is not linear
> algebra. 

[ Neither is ours, but on the other hand, we try to avoid transcendentals
  as the plague - they're expensive on almost any machine ]

Because the Alpha's don't have native instructions for sin/cos/exp, all 
depends on the (glibc) library implementation - the compiler can't really do 
much here (unless we started to inline "better" code than there is to be 
found in glibc, but that seems to be putting the horse behind the cart).

> SPECfp apparently does not test sin/cos/exp; a very stupid choice in 
> my opinion, but others vigorously defend it.

Self-imposed limitation - they don't want to have benchmark code that spends 
a significant time in libraries (probably to prevent all sorts of 
uncontrollable special-case tuning) - see their web page, 
http://www.spec.org.

