How much extra work must one do to take advantage of the second
CPU for number-crunching under SMP Linux?  We have a 533-MHz
Linux/Alpha box from SWT which we use to run a lot of Fourier
transform code, among other things, and it stomples our 175-MHz
R4400 SGI Indy into the dust -- it's literally at least a factor
of 3-5 faster.

Considering the SpecFP available from a single processor only,
one sees about a 50% increase moving from the PII/400 to the
Alpha/533.  I see your point about the $/SpecFP, though.  I
might have to try buying a dual PII system next ....

> Did I miss something? Is there any other than religious reason
> to use Alpha based boxes for number crunching? What must be taken
> into consideration is the status of Linux/Alpha vs. Linux/Intel
> and the problems getting code from available tools (gcc, g77) that
> shows all the MFLOPS of an Linux/Alpha system (we write our stuff
> in C/C++ using gcc 2.7.2.x)

That is a *serious* consideration.  A coworker of mine experienced
a show-stopping error in which his program would dump core on an
innocuous call if he linked in the standard C++ library (this is
under glibc-based Red Hat Linux/Alpha v5.0).  He had to start
using EGCS to get things to run properly (which they eventually
did).  Also, we're having a hard time finding Motif for glibc-based
Linux/Alpha.

On the other hand, I'm not sure what the status of Linux/SMP for
Intel processors is -- there may be problems just as or more
severe than the ones we've been having with Linux/Alpha.

> Looks like everytime we try - we won't get Alphas into the Lab?
> Any helpful advices will be appreciated.

We've had our system for about 3 months, and even considering the
limitations on available development tools, we love it.  It's
greatly increased our ability to perform floating-point calculcations
on large datasets.

-Brian

