lsr@snerd.ddns.org wrote:
:   Peter A Fein <p-fein@uchicago.edu> wrote:
: >
: > First, which OS are you using, as this is probably going to make a
: > difference in how you access this memory.  I don't know anything about
: > OSF, but in linux, user programs can't access real memory locations
: > b/c of virtual memory.  You'd need to write a kernel-level driver
: > which provided a user interface to the hardware.  I have heard some
: > talk of adding user-level direct adressing, but I don't believe it's
: > going to be implemented any time soon.
: >
: > --

: I guess I should have been a little more specific in my request.  I understand
: virtual memory vs physical memory etc.  My question should have been:  If I
: have an ISA card that is setup via jumpers for memory mapped IO beggining at
: address 0xFC0000, can I use that address as the offset in the mmap function
: call, or is there some translation that I must do.  I'm interested in both
: Linux and DEC OSF.  Thanks.

 For Linux on Intel machines, it's fairly simple to write a device
driver to map a card into user space. For a DEC Alpha, things are
more complicated. DEC Unix (and OSF, I believe) do not support the
mmap() call "officially".  I have a driver for our card that
maps the card into user space via mmap().  It may help you.
The Linux driver is at ftp://cmr.ncsl.nist.gov/pub/multikron/mk_Linux.tar.Z,
(for Intel machines), while the DEC Unix/Alpha driver is at
ftp://cmr.ncsl.nist.gov/pub/multikron/mk_Alpha.tar.Z. 
For DEC Unix system, the user program doesn't access the card
via the virtual address directly; there are some translation
macros that must be used. These macros are in one of the include
files in the tar file I gave above.

Good luck,

WJS

