In article <356454FC.120448B8@radixtek.com>,
Eric Monsler  <eric_monsler@radixtek.com> wrote:
>Also, is it only the latest/greatest Digital compiler that is
>significantly faster than GCC/egcs?  I ask because I wonder if any older
>server machines (say 166-266 MHz boxes) might be leaving use, and still
>have valid licenses.

For my code (almost no floating point, extensive use of 64-bit integers),
the OSF compiler (standard compiler for OSF 3.x) was slightly faster than
the DEC C compiler (standard compiler for DU 4.x), in maximum optimizing
mode. This is the code that I found to be 20-30% slower with GCC. It
is an emulator for a Cyber CPU (60-bit one's complement machine; only
floating point operation I use is a normalize, all Cyber floating point
operations are done with integer operations, including multiplication
and division, in order to get the rounding done correctly).



