vogt@127.0.0.1 wrote:

: I have a 266MHz 21064. Compiling a kernel is slower as on a 133 pentium

Unless you are cross-compiling Alpha kernel on Pentium this is a totally
meaningless comparison.  Producing 64-bit RISC code, and optimizing it,
differs substantially from making something like that for 32-bit CISC.
A compiler may have the same name but this is quite a bit different
animal - even assuming that gcc for Alpha is as mature as that one
for Intel (which is not the case).

Saying that comparing speed of processors by looking at "bare" clock
rates is not very informative either.  RISC chips, like Alpha, have
to perform many small instructions to the same job as their CISC
counterparts so they have to run at higher clock rates for a similar
results.

This is still not the whole story.  Even within Intel-like family
a direct comparison of clock rates between, say, Intel and Cyrix
gives you only a very rough indication of a relative performance.
On the other end  LX Alpha boards substantially outperform SX - both
running at a nominal 533 MHz.  There are other things but a processor.

Still I agree that $300 for a "bare-bones" UDB does not sound like
a very exciting deal.  Once properly populated ($$$) UDB is equivalent
to 60-90 MHz Pentium - very much depends on what you are doing with it
and how you "filled" it.  Still you have on-board SCSI controller,
decent ethernet, TGA video.  How much this is worth for you depends
on what are your plans for it.

  --mj



