>>>>> "l" == lejar  <lejar@gte.net> writes:

    l>   I have read the FAQ until I am blue in the face.  The way it
    l> reads you need to have a system up and running to make the
    l> aboot disk.  Can I boot directly from the SRM >>> prompt?  Do
    l> you know if you really need MILO?

The aboot disk?  MILO?  What are you talking about?

Do you mean starting MILO from SRM, then starting Linux from MILO?
Well, MILO isn't necessary, if you have the correct kernel image.  You
can use SRM to boot Linux (which I recommend doing).  If you want to
use SRM to boot Linux, you'll need to use BSD disklabels instead of
fdisk-style partitioning if you want to be able to boot from your hard
disk.

If you're at the install stage, all you gotta do is create the
appropriate boot disk (e.g. for the AS200, that would be avanti-s.img
if you're using Red Hat Linux), execute 'boot dva0 -file "vmlinux.gz"
-flags "root=/dev/fd0 load_ramdisk=1"' from SRM, and be certain to
partition with minlabel (not Disk Druid or fdisk's BSD mode).  To get
around in the install, go ahead and run fdisk on the target disk.
Then switch to another window and execute 'minlabel /tmp/DISK', where
DISK is replaced with the appropriate device name (e.g. sda).

Here's how I configured my system:

	- partition a: 2.5MB 'boot' partition (room for aboot and a
	  kernel)
	- partition b: 128MB 'swap' partition
	- partition c: entire disk
	- partition d: 50MB 'ext2fs' root file system
	- partition e: 1GB 'ext2fs' /usr file system
	- partition f: 3GB 'ext2fs' /var file system

After the installation, I configured aboot using the following command:
	'swriteboot -f1 -f3 /dev/DISK /boot/bootlx'
Where DISK is replaced with the appropriate device name (e.g. sda).
The '-f1' and '-f3' switches were necessary because partitions 1 and 3
overlapped the boot area (sector 0 and following).

You can optionally add a pathname to a compressed (or uncompressed)
kernel image to the end of that command line, and it will store that
image in the boot record (which you access using the BOOT command
switch '-file "0/vmlinux"'):
	'swriteboot -f1 -f3 /dev/DISK /boot/bootlx /vmlinux.gz'

Er, does that help any?

