We (again) have the problem of choosing components for some
Unix-WS for scientific calculations (number crunching, mostly FP,
Monte Carlo simulations of large molecular assemblies). 

The last two years we worked on lattice Monte Carlo and I had a 
bunch of PPro/200's running, which did very good job on INTEGER 
stuff (remember: one PPro/200 is effectively 80% the calc speed of 
SGI-O2(R10K/195) in Integer calculations).

Now it happened that we got our FP-Monte Carlo code done and
we think about extension of our computer cluster. With the advent
of newer intel chips, it's very hard to decide for an Alpha-Cluster
(what was originally planned!).

What we can afford is a bunch of boxes for say 42K Deutschmark ($ 22K)
That would be enough for (todays prices) about 8 Dual PII/400 including
4.3 or 6.5Gig HD and 128M RAM w/o monitors.

The Spec suite says that one PII/400 comes with  about 16 SpecINT 
and more than 12 SpecFP (in NT on BX board). 
Lets get more concrete:

PII/400		$770
PII/400		$770 (Dual setup)
MoBo (Tyan1832)	$260
--------------------
		$1800
SpecFP		24
--------------------
$75 / SpecFP


533-Alpha (Microway)
Lx-MoBo(1MB L3)
Mid-Tower	$1795
---------------------
		$1795
SpecFP		17
---------------------
$105 / SpecFP

Did I miss something? Is there any other than religious reason
to use Alpha based boxes for number crunching? What must be taken
into consideration is the status of Linux/Alpha vs. Linux/Intel 
and the problems getting code from available tools (gcc, g77) that 
shows all the MFLOPS of an Linux/Alpha system (we write our stuff
in C/C++ using gcc 2.7.2.x)

Looks like everytime we try - we won't get Alphas into the Lab? 
Any helpful advices will be appreciated.

Mirco

BTW, who has some kind of a table that states how 21164A's performance
depends on the L3 or mainboard.



