>More to the point for this group... I am thinking of setting up my
>UDB to triple boot:
>
>Digital UNIX 40.B
>NT 4.0 Server (or maybe JPN NT4.0 Server)
>Linux (RedHat 4.2 - is 5.0 solid enough?)
>
>Any words of wisdom, gotchas, whatevers....

There's a big gotcha.

Digital Unix wants to use the SRM firmware.  NT wants to use the ARC
firmware.  Both are present in an UDB, but it's a little strange and
contorted to switch back and forth between them.

Also, Digital Unix and NT have different partitioning schemes (NT on
an Alpha uses the same old DOS partitioning mechanism as NT on x86).

I know Linux can co-exist with NT, using the ARC firmware and the DOS
partitioning scheme.  I'm fairly sure Linux can also be booted from
SRM on the UDB (I know it can boot from SRM on other Alphas).

If you really want all three, my reccomendation would be to use at
least two different SCSI disks, and use ARC for both NT and Linux,
having Linux use the familiar old DOS partitioning scheme that almost
all x86 Linux users use.  Running Linux from the ARC firmware using
MILO is very easy once you've got it set up.

