I'm involved in a project to port some code to 64 bit architectures and
we are getting pretty good results on an R10000 200MHz processor.  The
same code compiled with gcc on our Alpha 533 runs about the same speed. 
We have put optimisations in to utilise two pipelines although the SGI
compiler does do re-ordering of instructions for itself too.

I guess, considering the 21164 has two integer pipelines like the MIPS
and more than double the clock speed we would have expected about double
the performance but we're not seeing that.

I tried egcs but the code was actually slower! :-(

We don't have a license for DEC UNIX so that option is out by the way.

I guess the other option is to optimise the main loop of the code by
hand and in that case I could do with some pointers to details of the
processor instruction set including clock timings and the likes, so far
I have only been able to find the most basic information.  Trying to
download anything from digital is a nightmare, talk about slow!!!

I do have to say I am generally very pleased with the performance of the
machine though and am asking for this so I can really make the thing
hum! :-)

-- 
Dr. Shane Sturrock - sss@holyrood.ed.ac.uk
