                DEC 300 XL Boot/Install/Library Questions

Let me start by saying that this is a VERY useful newsgroup.  It reminds
me of comp.sys.sun back around 1990 when the signal-to-noise ratio was
still very high.

I have just bought forty-eight  300XL's for a Beowulf system, and have a
few questions for you AXP linux gurus.  We are starting off with RedHat
4.2 .  If you are interested, here is a detailed description of our
project.

   * (1)  Does anyone know how to set a DEC DE-500 100-base T card to
     run at full duplex and NOT negotiate?  I assume this is an ifconfig
     control command.  Or do more recent versions of the drivers than
     those in the 2.0.30 kernel handle negotiation better?
   * Our machines have AlphaBios firmware.  We are using it to load
     linload.exe from a DOS partition on the disk, which then loads
     milo from the same, passing it the necessary parameters to either
     boot from the network or from a kernel on the disk. The system is
     set up with the latter being the "primary" boot choice.  If we push
     the "soft-reset" button on the front panel, the machine reboots
     cleanly, ending with a login: prompt. But there are two annoying
     (x48)problems with this:
        o We would like root to be able to cleanly reboot from the
          command line.  But when we issue a "reboot" command as root,
          the system halts and does not reboot. Also,the system will not
          boot without a keyboard plugged in (AlphaBios complains).
     Could anyone suggest a solution for this?  Would any of the
     following work?
        o Idea 1: Use aboot rather than milo?
        o Idea 2: I have seen references to loading milo into "flash
          memory" .  Does this mean replacing AlphaBios with MILO or
          something like that?  Could someone please post some detailed
          directions about how to load milo into "flash memory" on a
          300XL
        o Idea 3: Would it be hard to write a 10 or 20 line C program
          which is executed as the final line of the shutdown script,
          and which sends a hardware interrupt to the system, the same
          as is generated by pushing the "soft-reboot" button on the
          front panel?  Does someone know what register to set or what
          interrupt or signal to generate?
        o Idea 4:  Would replacing AlphaBios with SRM solve any either
          of these annoying problems?
   * We were going to experiment with RedHat 5.0 but the RedHat
     installation scripts failed miserably.  It complained about the
     wrong architecture executables (yes we were using the alpha 5.0
     disk from powertools).  We now believe that this may be because the
     hardware clock appears to be set to 2018, when viewed by linux.
     Our theory is that this confuses the rpm package manager.  Is this
     correct? It is not possible to set the clock so that it appears to
     be any earlier than 2002 to linux, unless we set it back to (again
     as seen by linux) 1976.  So we though that the following might
     work:
        o Set clock so that linux thinks it is 1976.
        o Do a very minimal install of 5.0 (ie, select NO optional
          packages)
        o Set the date of the system correctly.
        o Now install lots of other packages.
     So the question is: will this help solve our 5.0 installation
     woes?  I gather that this clock question has generated lots of
     discussion.  It's not a problem for us except at installation time
     -- after installation we simply get the time from a reliable
     time-server at bootup.
   * A final question: I would like to use a set of routines from DEC's
     optimized "Extended Math Library" (DXML) for AXP/OSF.  I know that
     I can compile with the non_shared flag on the OSF system, then
     simply move the executable and run it on my linux box.  I have
     tried this and it works fine, but it's inconvenient.  Is it
     possible to simply move a collection of libraries from OSF to my
     linux box and link to those?  I tried with just the DXML library,
     but it makes use of many other OSF AXP library calls that are not
     in the linux libraries, so this failed because of unresolved
     symbols.  Has anyone sucessfully copied a set of libraries from OSF
     AXP onto linux and linked to those with gcc?  If so, please could
     you post something describing what files did you move, where did
     you put them, and how did you link to them?

