<HTML>

<CENTER>
<H1>
DEC&nbsp;300&nbsp;XL&nbsp;Boot/Install/Library Questions</H1></CENTER>
Let me start by saying that this is a VERY&nbsp;useful newsgroup.&nbsp;
It reminds me of comp.sys.sun back around 1990 when the signal-to-noise
ratio was still very high.

<P>I&nbsp;have just bought forty-eight&nbsp; 300XL's for a Beowulf system,
and have a few questions for you AXP linux gurus.&nbsp; We are starting
off with RedHat 4.2 .&nbsp; If you are interested, here is a <A HREF="http://www.lsc-group.phys.uwm.edu/~www/docs/beowulf/index.html">detailed
description of our project.</A>
<UL>
<LI>
(1)&nbsp; Does anyone know how to set a DEC DE-500 100-base T card to run
at full duplex and NOT&nbsp;negotiate?&nbsp; I assume this is an <B>ifconfig</B>
control command.&nbsp; Or do more recent versions of the drivers than those
in the 2.0.30 kernel handle negotiation better?</LI>

<LI>
Our machines have AlphaBios firmware.&nbsp; We are using it to load <B>linload.exe</B>
from a DOS&nbsp;partition on the disk, which then loads <B>milo&nbsp;</B>from
the same, passing it the necessary parameters to either boot from the network
or from a kernel on the disk. The system is set up with the latter being
the "primary" boot choice.&nbsp; If we push the "soft-reset" button on
the front panel, the machine reboots cleanly, ending with a <B>login: </B>prompt.
But there are two annoying (x48)problems with this:</LI>

<UL>
<LI>
We would like root to be able to cleanly reboot from the command line.&nbsp;
But <FONT COLOR="#FF0000">when we issue a "reboot" command as root, the
system halts and does not reboot</FONT>. Also,the <FONT COLOR="#FF0000">system
will not boot without a keyboard plugged in</FONT> (AlphaBios complains).</LI>
</UL>
Could anyone suggest a solution for this?&nbsp; Would any of the following
work?
<UL>
<LI>
<FONT COLOR="#FF0000">Idea 1</FONT>:&nbsp;Use aboot rather than milo?</LI>

<LI>
<FONT COLOR="#FF0000">Idea 2</FONT>:&nbsp;I have seen references to loading
<B>milo</B>&nbsp;into "flash memory" .&nbsp; Does this mean replacing AlphaBios
with MILO or something like that?&nbsp; Could someone please <FONT COLOR="#FF0000">post
some detailed directions about how to load <B>milo</B></FONT>&nbsp;into
"flash memory" on a 300XL</LI>

<LI>
<FONT COLOR="#FF0000">Idea 3</FONT>:&nbsp;Would it be hard to write a 10
or 20 line C program which is executed as the final line of the shutdown
script, and which sends a hardware interrupt to the system, the same as
is generated by pushing the "soft-reboot" button on the front panel?&nbsp;
Does someone know what register to set or what interrupt or signal to generate?</LI>

<LI>
<FONT COLOR="#FF0000">Idea 4</FONT>:&nbsp; Would replacing AlphaBios with
SRM&nbsp;solve any either of these annoying problems?</LI>
</UL>

<LI>
We were going to experiment with RedHat 5.0 but the RedHat installation
scripts failed miserably.&nbsp; It complained about the wrong architecture
executables (yes we were using the alpha 5.0 disk from powertools).&nbsp;
We now believe that this may be because the hardware clock appears to be
set to 2018, when viewed by linux.&nbsp; <FONT COLOR="#000000">Our theory
is that this confuses the rpm package manager</FONT>.&nbsp; <FONT COLOR="#FF0000">Is
this correct?</FONT> It is not possible to set the clock so that it appears
to be any earlier than 2002 to linux, unless we set it back to (again as
seen by linux) 1976.&nbsp; So we though that the following <I>might </I>work:</LI>

<UL>
<LI>
Set clock so that linux thinks it is 1976.</LI>

<LI>
Do a very minimal install of 5.0 (ie, select NO&nbsp;optional packages)</LI>

<LI>
Set the date of the system correctly.</LI>

<LI>
Now install lots of other packages.</LI>
</UL>
So the <FONT COLOR="#FF0000">question is: will this help solve our 5.0
installation woes</FONT>?&nbsp; I gather that this clock question has generated
lots of discussion.&nbsp; It's not a problem for us except at installation
time -- after installation we simply get the time from a reliable time-server
at bootup.
<LI>
A final question: I&nbsp;would like to use a set of routines from DEC's&nbsp;
optimized "Extended Math Library"&nbsp;(DXML) for AXP/OSF.&nbsp; I know
that I can compile with the non_shared flag on the OSF system, then simply
move the executable and run it on my linux box.&nbsp; I have tried this
and it works fine, but it's inconvenient.&nbsp; Is it possible to simply
move a collection of libraries from OSF to my linux box and link to those?&nbsp;
I tried with just the DXML&nbsp;library, but it makes use of many other
OSF AXP library calls that are not in the linux libraries, so this failed
because of unresolved symbols.&nbsp; Has anyone sucessfully copied a set
of libraries from OSF AXP onto linux and linked to those with gcc?&nbsp;
If so, please could you <FONT COLOR="#FF0000">post something describing
what files did you move, where did you put them, and how did you link to
them</FONT>?</LI>
</UL>
Thanks in advance for any replies.&nbsp; We have already learned a great
deal from this newsgroup, and hope to contribute something in return --
we are currently writing a set of <A HREF="http://www.lsc-group.phys.uwm.edu/~www/docs/beowulf/index.html">web
pages</A> which we will post with the complete instructions for how we
built our systems.&nbsp; I hope that this eventually proves useful for
anyone with 300XL boxes.

<P>Cheers,
<BR>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Bruce Allen</HTML>

